#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3fc7e6-b73c-5d57-94a3-97e633c74805" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Congruences by Trial and Error"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3a0b0e-a348-594e-bc5d-1acbeb411c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-14590" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $2x \\equiv 1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5)$ using trial and error." :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=5$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x = 0,1,2,3,4$ into the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & ≡2≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & ≡4≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times  \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & ≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & ✓ \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & ≡8≡5+3≡3≢1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is is $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}{3}} \\:(\\text{mod}\\:5).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $3$ modulo $5$ is a solution. For example, note that $8\\equiv 3,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡2\\cdot 8\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡16\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 5+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+1\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡1\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203681 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cabdfa53-93e3-5825-bd83-6d8a1bf43be4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "c522c4d0-6266-54d9-9254-84659316a144" :question/math-academy-id "e-14591" :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $7x \\equiv 3 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:15).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=15,$ so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,15)=1.\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡3\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 7≡28≡15+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {7}^{4} & ≡13\\cdot 7≡91≡6\\cdot 15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $13 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15),$ which means that $13$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $15$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $13,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 13\\cdot 7x & ≡13\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ (13\\cdot 7)x & ≡39 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 15+9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:15).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203682 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fccbe8e1-4491-598d-b4ee-fcfeea325950" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/id #uuid "ad19d1cb-08fe-5a73-8ba7-65a5f038f9fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14592" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $15x \\equiv 17 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:44).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=44,$ where \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,44)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ we use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}44 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 14 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 14\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =44-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =15-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =15-14 \\\\  & =15-(44-15\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $44,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 3-44 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 15\\cdot 3 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:44),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $44$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 3\\cdot 15x & ≡3\\cdot 17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ (3\\cdot 15)x & ≡51 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡44+7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44) \\\\ x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 7 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:44).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "867713da-e1d0-5629-8059-3ae2848d7e96" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Additive and Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "b2442e76-8836-596a-85ad-232ed18aaa5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14662" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n7x - 11 \\equiv 29 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:19)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax\\equiv b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-11 & ≡29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x-11+11 & ≡29+11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡40\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡38+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\cdot 19+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 7x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=7$ and $n=19$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(7,19)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19).$ To find the inverse of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$, we compute the exponents of $7\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:19)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{2} & ≡49≡2\\cdot 19+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ {7}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 7≡77≡4\\cdot 19+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $11 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:19),$ which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $7$ (modulo $19$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 11\\cdot 7x & ≡11\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ (11\\cdot 7)x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡19+3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19) \\\\ x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:19).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cabdfa53-93e3-5825-bd83-6d8a1bf43be4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d34f638-9a0e-5ca4-a67d-6e32e086b9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419796} {:answer/id #uuid "680844b9-8e08-5d3b-87b4-0ea3de54ee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419802} {:answer/id #uuid "b95b1927-2dd5-58b1-bd34-01d6ec3c0908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419811} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419814} {:answer/id #uuid "f740ef79-86a1-5132-98b5-754715a1cf95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f78442-1c48-529e-80f1-950ea1923966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6233de2-e84d-5538-a9c5-3e6781767893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b8f3c46-66e9-51ef-a1f9-2699e1171085" :question/math-academy-id "q-132139" :question/problem "Solve the modular equation $4x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=11$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,11)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot 4≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 3\\cdot 4x & ≡3\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (3\\cdot 4)x & ≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡1\\cdot 11+10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fccbe8e1-4491-598d-b4ee-fcfeea325950" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419801} {:answer/id #uuid "69e7f375-a389-5bdc-89d6-4d38cccfbe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡32\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419803} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddb8243-ef75-5cc7-9d5f-07daa908cd86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡40\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419804} {:answer/id #uuid "882df7c4-3a86-5d50-8e6d-f1ec40ab280a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419805} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1c70ff-3515-5c02-9286-ee2663506546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡43\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b9f08f-ccc6-5559-9c89-4b109a747d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419801} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ef70e3-6162-577a-bcdf-82f0147aac4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b5eee82-5740-5d92-bedf-cc94230c0e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-132295" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $35x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=35$ and $n=51$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(35,51)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$ To find the inverse of $35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.  \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}51 & = & 35\\cdot 1 & + & 16 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 16\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 16 & = & 3\\cdot 5 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =51-35\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3 & =35-16\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =16-3\\cdot 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =16-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-(35-16\\cdot 2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(51-35)\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11-35\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $51,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51\\cdot 11+35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot (51-16) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $35\\cdot 35≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51),$ which means that \n$35$  is its own multiplicative inverse ( (modulo $51$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $35,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 35\\cdot 35x & ≡35\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ (35\\cdot 35)x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 51+22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51) \\\\ x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,51).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cabdfa53-93e3-5825-bd83-6d8a1bf43be4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45591275-4f92-5c6a-95be-4ec70c990168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419800} :answer-field/id #uuid "814560d3-f6d1-50be-89f1-2bd68351a648" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ebcbf3-35d1-5715-bd12-20face8731a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419794} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6157f91-75b7-5dab-8ec1-dce3b7296f9a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41993f1-642c-526e-83fb-2308acfe075f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419807} :answer-field/id #uuid "3844c75c-d587-5008-a9b3-3c8338f4604d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088c8755-232f-5d67-b714-cb5f32d8ba5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419793} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e12290b-ad74-561b-b9eb-3a1ed0857885" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad76e3b5-29b9-5bea-a8d9-e0706604d1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419809} :answer-field/id #uuid "6575a794-6cba-5e75-b6ca-f3db8d3388a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dff76ff1-2195-54e0-b517-736d56f4a1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-213469" :question/problem "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25.$\n\n${6}^{2}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${6}^{3}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${6}^{4}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n${6}^{5}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-4}}\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-5}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=6$ and $n=25$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,25)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ To find the inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$, we compute the exponents of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36≡25+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 6≡66≡2\\cdot 25+16≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{4} & ≡16\\cdot 6≡96≡3\\cdot 25+21≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{5} & ≡21\\cdot 6≡126≡5\\cdot 25+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $21\\cdot 6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25),$ which means that $21$ is the multiplicative inverse of $6$ (modulo $25$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $21,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 21\\cdot 6x & ≡21\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ (21\\cdot 6)x & ≡63 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 25+13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ x & ≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "867713da-e1d0-5629-8059-3ae2848d7e96" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Additive and Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44378403-b1a6-51ab-bdb4-8a83dc56f909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419798} {:answer/id #uuid "a57931da-2515-5c40-8190-f20e4d544a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419808} {:answer/id #uuid "c0555944-5da1-5f24-9981-3b0e3a814a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419812} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fd8304-33a3-582c-8a4f-dbb98a08be96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419816} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e32835-b8b0-5ef3-9fcc-e5ddf1c98c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419818} :answer-field/id #uuid "92526c99-58f2-5c30-97c0-6d85b023cfa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3db815a9-d784-52dd-bf7e-2dc644a57255" :question/math-academy-id "q-213507" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5x+4≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & ≡23\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x+4-4 & ≡23-4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡1\\cdot 13+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=5$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(5,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡1\\cdot 13+12≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡12\\cdot 5≡60≡4\\cdot 13+8≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 5≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $8$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $8,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 8\\cdot 5x & ≡8\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (8\\cdot 5)x & ≡48 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡9+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "867713da-e1d0-5629-8059-3ae2848d7e96" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using Additive and Multiplicative Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea6e5b-5c39-5cf4-a983-f88e1861ecd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419815} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b43649-64c4-5c3e-ae9c-72a41cf3ebb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b2d85f9-70d5-5d8a-aa2a-9b3b30df44ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-213522" :question/problem "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n4x+3≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),\n$$\n\nexpressing your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & ≡34\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x+3-3 & ≡34-3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡31\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡2\\cdot 13+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡1\\cdot 13+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡12\\cdot 4≡48≡3\\cdot 13+9≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡36≡2\\cdot 13+10≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{6} & ≡10\\cdot 4≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $10\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $10$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $10,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 10\\cdot 4x & ≡10\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (10\\cdot 4)x & ≡50 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡11+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3fc7e6-b73c-5d57-94a3-97e633c74805" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Congruences by Trial and Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bbe80cc-77d0-5bec-b9a3-584c08cefb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419795} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2633ee-1d5b-5783-bb62-c0caed17ffa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c11ba83-6848-5a89-900b-5fb2ae872fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214736" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $5x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $6.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 1 & ≡5≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & ≡10≡6+4≡4≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 6+3≡3≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times  \\\\ 5\\cdot 4 & ≡20≡3\\cdot 6+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & ✓ \\\\ 5\\cdot 5 & ≡25≡4\\cdot 6+1≡1≢2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $4$ modulo $6$ is a solution. For example, note that $10≡4,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡5\\cdot 10\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡50\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 6+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3fc7e6-b73c-5d57-94a3-97e633c74805" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Congruences by Trial and Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "424edbb9-4ced-5f21-bbeb-ac5e675871e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419797} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9f0ad8-9183-59f0-8835-5839bbca3f42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f60f67b-c6e2-55b6-a159-dee4ac2e0060" :question/math-academy-id "q-214738" :question/problem "Solve the linear congruence $3x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $7.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=7$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & ≡3≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & ≡6≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & ≡9≡7+2≡2≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & ≡12≡7+5≡5≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 7+1≡1≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 6 & ≡18≡2\\cdot 7+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $6$ modulo $7$ is a solution. For example, note that $13≡6,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡3\\cdot 13\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡39\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 7+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fccbe8e1-4491-598d-b4ee-fcfeea325950" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Congruences Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44efd338-666a-5ada-a785-765da28084d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419799} {:answer/id #uuid "925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419806} {:answer/id #uuid "b19af155-55c5-534a-b966-b4cb8b712651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419810} {:answer/id #uuid "d256f5a7-e3af-5e6b-8f30-286d8ceacb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡17\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419813} {:answer/id #uuid "f4efd977-d8da-5378-8891-6af2bd5f45d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "925492cf-b212-5f24-a956-5e4ebd50534c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72cedeb-72f7-5d00-aef1-922a6cd3715e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fe75766-c260-5668-9726-1bdf3c8f2f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-78069" :question/problem "Solve the congruence $15x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=43$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,43)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm. \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}43 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 13\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 2\\cdot 6 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =43-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =15-13 \\\\ 1 & =13-2\\cdot 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13-(15-13)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(43-15\\cdot 2)\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $43,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (43-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15\\cdot 23≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43),$ which means that $23$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $43$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $23,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 23\\cdot 15x & ≡23\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ (23\\cdot 15)x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$"}]}
